[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: Campus DNS resolver on srv1 (BIND/named). Wireless access/gateway path (aswa1 → VLAN 40 → dswa2 SVI 10.6.0.1) is healthy.
- Mechanism: The laptop receives DNS server 10.5.0.4 (srv1). With recursion disabled, BIND returns REFUSED for any name it is not authoritative for. Applications that depend on DNS appear to “not work,” even though IP connectivity and default routing are fine.
- Localization: 
  • Laptop1 has IP 10.6.0.100/24, default via 10.6.0.1; it pings the gateway and other subnets successfully (pc1/pc2/pc3 OK) and reaches upstream (traceroute to 216.239.35.4 via 10.6.0.3 → 10.0.0.61 → r1 → 203.0.113.1).
  • Laptop’s resolver is 10.5.0.4 and queries to it are REFUSED with “recursion requested but not available” (dig @10.5.0.4 google.com → REFUSED). 
  • On srv1, named is listening on UDP/53, and /etc/bind/named.conf.options explicitly sets “recursion no;”. Authoritative zone jeremysitlab.com answers, but anything else is refused.
- Root cause: Recursion was disabled in srv1’s BIND configuration, so clients cannot resolve external or unqualified names.
- Corrective fix: Re-enable recursion on srv1 and limit it to internal subnets. Example in /etc/bind/named.conf.options:
  options { recursion yes; allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; 192.0.2.0/24; }; listen-on { any; }; dnssec-validation no; };
  Optionally add forwarders to reachable upstream resolvers if required in this lab. Then restart named (systemctl restart named). Verify with dig @10.5.0.4 google.com returning an answer. Wireless gateway requires no change.

=== END ===